        "tipo": "gramatica",
        "min": 12,
        "titulo": "-er verbs in the present tense",
        "explicacao": "STEP 1: almost all verbs that end in «-er» follow the same pattern. Remove «-er» from the infinitive and add: «-e, -es, -e, -ons, -ez, -ent». Example: «parler → je parle, tu parles, il parle, nous parlons, vous parlez, ils parlent». STEP 2: in speech, «je parle», «tu parles», «il parle» and «ils parlent» sound the same («parl»). Only «nous parlons» (par-LOHN) and «vous parlez» (par-LAY) sound different. That is why spelling matters. STEP 3: before a vowel or a silent «h», «je» becomes «j'»: «j'habite», «j'aime», «j'étudie», «j'écoute». STEP 4: «on» uses the form of «il»: «On parle français». STEP 5: «parler» + language takes no article: «Je parle français.» STEP 6: after «aimer», use the verb in the infinitive: «J'aime danser.» Articles come in lesson 5. STEP 7: this pattern covers about 90% of French verbs. A few have small spelling changes («nous mangeons»), which we will see later.",
        "comparacao": "English verbs barely change (I speak, you speak, she speaks), while French writes six forms and pronounces four of them almost the same. The written endings are «-e, -es, -e, -ons, -ez, -ent».",
